<!-- cell: salient-concept id="8e8a6674-478c-419d-896c-90a260f85822" title="The traveller" words="050b7c47-f1d5-4cc9-be63-4f6dc4483660:17-29, 8a55b6b4-4676-4603-9027-9bd910fa6191:120-144, 3879704f-4731-49d0-9f0e-f680a210613e:145-169, 35e606b6-0b30-45aa-9de4-0e05d32a8cb5:183-190" -->

A lone alien stranded on Mars after a crash, who only wants to survive until its people come for it. Hurt by beings it cannot understand, it calls for help.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5f9cd3d4-c51b-4dc6-bdc7-776861df77da" title="The alien race" words="07d7df7a-142f-49bd-8b74-195d2cf2156d:30-41, 4cbe19e8-cad8-480e-a0c8-9fed365054c5:191-203, b04e6690-be33-4edc-9167-3f4f88de4afc:366-390, ca4dd131-6d60-4eb5-94e6-66a6b74930bd:391-399" -->

A people who come for one of their own, and treat whoever is hurting it as the enemy.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c1bf1836-9a18-4db7-8df5-798d9b55fb73" title="Humanity" words="803e7f0f-3f7d-400b-8d23-a7ad1ee72e84:7-15, 0085edeb-7752-4c6a-89a8-97c8e0b69bb1:93-105, 931f009e-f61c-4da1-8a3c-cf27c0c4508e:106-119, a9fe2a9f-ea80-415c-8288-b465ac70392e:218-224, 17eaf369-53a9-40bc-9979-329c1dc15d35:324-351, e9a97711-44b1-4bc8-afbd-96ebbd53ee58:404-422" -->

The side that hurts a living being without knowing it is alive, and then takes its people's arrival and strike for an attack.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="832fea15-0d66-42e4-9299-35ef692642ee" title="The faster-than-light link" words="569dda37-7121-4712-9a0d-458a9673949a:170-182, a9f49cfd-bd85-4b39-b31f-bb5fecbac173:352-365" -->

The traveller's only way to reach its people. Through it a stranding becomes a rescue, and an injury becomes a call to war.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="581dedf1-c744-401f-85cb-26bc4bcb5532" title="Misunderstanding" words="740df94b-2aae-4663-9ba2-ff2792d8e26d:43-55, fdb3c91d-d018-4dc4-ab55-205746aecc8b:237-270, 8ff4d31c-27b8-4c48-91a9-97855bc1f273:308-323, 49537012-4c24-49f4-8f84-9e951585e187:377-390, 3f9ba1f4-9b3d-4f20-bc87-4f1c3b317dd1:412-422" -->

Neither side can read the other, so each takes the other's actions for an attack.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2d8238ef-1765-48e7-b778-8d463b3d27df" title="War" words="d6c12376-13d3-4f79-9051-b363f68cf421:57-58, e0bef313-726c-4e9d-9674-1e23498ef86f:391-399, be31be95-c2dd-4501-b60e-a996d264d128:417-422" -->

How a war begins that no one meant to start.

<!-- cell: markdown id="e7ef96f4-592b-42dd-b994-760c7e1b5ea2" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, its windows yellow against the evening. Inside, the living room was small and lived-in: a worn sofa facing the television, a basket of folded laundry nobody had put away, a child's drawings taped crookedly to the wall beside a framed photograph of a launch pad at dawn. The smell of something baked earlier still hung faintly in the air, and the only other sound was the low murmur of the screen.

Captain Varn sat at one end of the sofa, still in the grey sweater she wore around the house, her daughter curled against her side with a blanket pulled up to her chin. The girl had been half asleep a moment before, but now she was sitting up, eyes fixed on the glow. Varn had not moved at all. One hand rested on her daughter's shoulder, and her fingers had gone still.

On the screen, the space agency's emblem filled the corner of the frame while the broadcast cut to a briefing room crowded with reporters. A spokesman stood at a podium before a wide display, and behind him, through a glass partition, scientists bent over their consoles, some talking quietly into headsets, others simply staring at their monitors. The spokesman cleared his throat and spoke carefully, as if each word had been weighed beforehand: the probe, drilling beneath the Martian surface, had detected something. The display behind him shifted to a grainy image, rust-red soil giving way to a darker shape below, too regular to dismiss. "We are not yet in a position to say what it is," he said. "Only that it is there."

The girl looked up at her mother. "Is that real?" she asked. Varn did not answer right away. She kept watching the screen, where the image of the buried shape held steady while the reporters' questions overlapped into noise, and her face showed nothing her daughter could read. At last she drew the blanket a little higher around the girl's shoulders. "It's real," she said quietly, and went on watching.

<!-- cell: markdown id="5034896d-ae03-4c27-817e-407375e2bdfa" -->

The plain stretched red and silent under a butterscotch sky, its rust-colored dust broken only by scattered basalt stones and the long shadows they cast in the thin morning light. The sun hung small and pale above the horizon, and a faint wind stirred the fine grit into slow, drifting veils that settled again as soon as they rose. At the center of the plain a shaft had been cut into the ground, a dark circle ringed with survey markers and the bright orange flags of a staked perimeter.

The excavation team arrived in two pressurized rovers that rolled to a stop at the edge of the markers, their wheels leaving deep ribbed tracks in the regolith. Figures in white suits climbed down, helmets catching the sunlight, and moved to their stations with the careful, bounding steps the low gravity demanded. One checked readings on a tablet strapped to a forearm; another walked the rim of the shaft, peering down into the dark. "Winch is clear," a voice crackled over the shared channel. "Ground's stable. We're good to lower."

A crane arm swung out over the opening, and from its cradle the robots descended one by one: squat, many-jointed machines with treaded bases and folded drill arms, their sensor lamps blinking blue as they dropped into the shaft on steel cables. The team watched the feeds on their wrist displays as the robots touched bottom, unclipped themselves, and unfolded. "All units down," someone reported. "Beginning excavation."

Below, the machines set to work. Drills whined to life and bit into the packed sediment, cutters scraped away layer after layer, and buckets of loosened earth were hauled up the shaft to be tipped onto a growing mound beside the rim. Dust plumed from the opening and drifted across the suited figures standing over it, who leaned in closer to their screens, watching the robots carve deeper into the ancient ground.

<!-- cell: markdown id="bb703cd3-90f0-435c-b207-363ef2febaba" -->

The command center lay deep beneath the surface, a long vault of grey concrete lit by the cold glow of a hundred screens. Officers in pale uniforms moved between the consoles, speaking in low voices into headsets, while a wall-sized display traced the slow arc of a red planet against the black. The air smelled of ozone and burnt coffee, and somewhere a printer chattered without pause.

Varn stood at the center of the room before a raised desk, her back straight, her hands folded behind her. An older officer with silver at his collar slid a thin case across the desk toward her and opened it. Inside lay a command key and an insignia bearing the outline of a small, sharp-prowed ship. "The corvette is yours," he said. "You'll escort the mission all the way to Mars. No one else knows the ship as you do." Varn looked at the insignia for a long moment, then took it and pinned it to her chest. A few of the nearby officers glanced up from their screens and nodded to her before turning back to their work.

Near the entrance, half-hidden behind a column, a girl of perhaps ten waited on a metal bench, swinging her legs and watching the red planet turn on the great screen. When Varn crossed the room toward her, the girl stood up quickly, her eyes going at once to the new insignia. Varn knelt so that their faces were level and took both the girl's hands in hers.

"I have to leave," she said quietly. "They've given me a ship, and I have to go with it." The girl's lip trembled, and she looked past her mother toward the screen, toward the red world turning there. Varn squeezed her hands and held her gaze, and around them the room went on humming, the officers bent over their consoles, the printer still chattering in the corner.

<!-- cell: markdown id="4547c385-2702-48b5-abfc-0c1765f754a4" -->

The plain stretched rust-red to a horizon that seemed too near, its dust lifted in thin veils by a wind that carried no sound through the helmets of those who worked there. The sky above was the pale butterscotch of a Martian afternoon, and the small sun hung in it like a coin seen through water. At the center of a staked-out grid, a pit had been cut into the regolith, its walls stepped and braced, its floor lit by portable lamps whose white glare made the iron-stained soil look almost black.

The crew moved slowly in their pressure suits, kneeling at the edges of the trench with brushes and handheld blowers, clearing the fine grit a few centimeters at a time. A drone hovered above the pit, its lenses turning to follow their hands. For hours the work had yielded nothing but layered sediment and the occasional pebble, logged and bagged out of habit. Then one of the excavators stopped, leaned closer, and raised a gloved hand. The others gathered at the rim.

Beneath the dust, a curved surface had begun to show. As the brushes worked outward, it took on a shape: a smooth, rounded form, like the shoulder of a figure, rising into what might have been the line of a bowed head. It looked carved, the way old stone sculptures look carved, but its surface did not behave like stone. Where the lamps struck it, it gave back a dark, oily sheen shot through with faint veins of something greenish and metallic. One of the team held a spectrometer to it, read the display, and read it again before passing it to the next pair of hands. The figures on the screen named elements found on Earth only in trace amounts, here bound together in quantities no one had expected.

Over the radio, the voices were quiet and clipped. "Keep brushing. Don't touch it with anything metal." "Get the full scan from above, every angle." "Is that tool marks along the edge, or erosion?" No one answered the last question. They went back to work around the object with even greater care, uncovering it grain by grain, while the drone circled overhead and the thin wind went on laying fresh dust along the lip of the trench.

<!-- cell: markdown id="e1a2ffb9-b4bc-480d-a8ed-6f10d010481e" -->

The house stood at the end of a gravel lane, low and whitewashed, its windows thrown open to the late afternoon. Inside, the rooms had the half-emptied look of a place someone was about to leave: a coat folded over the back of a chair, a single bag waiting by the door, the kitchen table cleared of everything but two mugs gone cold. Sunlight came in long and amber across the floorboards, and somewhere outside a dog barked once and fell quiet.

Varn knelt in the hallway so that her eyes were level with her daughter's. The girl had her arms crossed tightly over her chest, chin tucked down, as if holding herself together by force. Varn reached out and smoothed a strand of hair back behind the child's ear, then left her hand there, resting against her cheek.

"I know," Varn said softly, though the girl had not spoken. "I know it feels like forever." She drew a breath that caught a little halfway. "But listen to me. Every day. I'll talk to you every single day. Morning, night, whenever you want. You won't have time to miss me, I'll be in your ear so often you'll get sick of me."

The girl's mouth twitched, almost a smile, and then her face crumpled and she pressed herself into her mother's shoulder. Varn wrapped both arms around her and held on, rocking slightly, her eyes closed. For a long while neither of them moved. The light shifted on the wall, the bag sat by the door, and Varn kept murmuring the same promise into her daughter's hair, quietly, over and over, as if saying it enough times would make it hold.

<!-- cell: markdown id="526070f8-845b-4624-a40a-ee8f8509f959" -->

Mars turned slowly beneath the laboratory ship, a rust-colored curve streaked with the pale scars of old dust storms. The ship hung in the planet's shadow line, half of its long hull lit copper by the distant sun, half sunk in darkness. Its docking ring was open, and through the viewports along the spine the white glare of working lights spilled out into the vacuum. Alongside it, keeping station with careful bursts from its thrusters, came the corvette: smaller, leaner, its dark plating marked with the weathering of a long transit. The two vessels had crossed the last of the distance together, and now they settled into the same slow orbit as if tethered.

Varn stood at the corvette's forward observation port, hands clasped behind him, watching the surface. Far below, too small to see except as a faint glint where the light caught it, a lift capsule was rising from the excavation site, trailing a thin plume of exhaust that the thin air tore apart almost at once. His crew moved quietly at their stations behind him, calling out range and closing velocity in low voices. "First samples are clear of the atmosphere," one of them reported. Varn did not turn. "Then they're early," he said, and kept his eyes on the climbing spark.

The capsule met the laboratory ship's docking ring with a muffled clang that carried through the hull. Inside, technicians in sealed suits guided the sample containers out of the lock on magnetic sleds, moving them hand over hand along the guide rails toward the clean rooms. The containers were frosted with condensation, their seals stamped with the excavation team's markings. In the main laboratory, scientists gathered around the first opened case. Under the instruments lay fragments of the object: dark, dense, faintly ridged along their edges in a pattern too regular to have been made by the stone around them.

For a while no one spoke. Then the scanners began to hum, and the screens filled with readings, and the scientists bent closer, murmuring numbers to one another, calling for a second spectrometer, a finer blade, another light. One of them reached out with a gloved hand and turned a fragment slowly beneath the lens, watching the ridges catch and lose the light. Outside the viewports, the corvette's running lights blinked steadily against the dark, and Mars went on turning beneath them both.

<!-- cell: markdown id="b9acc691-3ee2-400b-9c59-fbfa44e6ebef" -->

The plain lay rust-red under a sky the colour of weak tea, and the wind moved fine dust in low sheets across the stones. At the foot of an eroded ridge, where the bedrock broke through the regolith in dark, layered slabs, a wheeled probe stood motionless with its sensor mast angled downward. A thin fan of light swept from its scanner across one slab, back and forth, over a pale inclusion no larger than a hand that sat in the stone like a knot in old wood.

The inclusion was not mineral. Threads finer than hair ran from it into the surrounding rock, branching through hairline fractures and pores, and along those threads something slow and faint was passing. When the probe had crept forward on its wheels, the threads nearest the surface had tightened by fractions of a millimetre, following the vibration. When the scanning beam crossed it, a dull shimmer had moved through the inclusion and outward along the threads, as if each pass of light were being measured and stored.

Down the slope, a pressurised rover rolled to a halt, its tracks crushing furrows into the dust. The hatch cycled, and the excavation team climbed out in white suits streaked orange at the knees, carrying cases of tools, coring drills, and folded sample frames. They moved carefully over the loose ground, their helmet lamps catching the ridge. One of them raised a gloved hand toward the probe, and another knelt to unpack a survey stake, speaking over the suit radio in a flat working voice: "Marker here. We'll grid it out from the probe's position."

Beneath their boots, through rock that had not been touched in an age, the threads drew taut once more. Each footfall reached the inclusion as a small tremor, and each tremor was met by that same faint shimmer. As the team drove the first stake into the regolith and began to lay out their lines, the thing in the stone held perfectly still, listening to every step.

<!-- cell: markdown id="a860471b-7b35-416f-90b2-6842f12f1ccb" -->

The station turned slowly above the night side of the planet, and through the long viewport of the observation ring the world below was a dark curve stitched with the amber threads of cities. The ring was quiet at that hour. A pair of technicians in grey coveralls drifted past the far hatch with a crate of filters tethered between them, murmuring about a pressure reading, and then they were gone and only the hum of the air recyclers remained.

Varn sat strapped loosely into a seat by the glass, a small screen cradled in her hands. Her hair, cut short for weightlessness, floated a little from her temples. She pressed the call and waited through the soft chime of the relay, watching the line of dawn creep toward the edge of the planet. Then the screen brightened, and a girl's face filled it, sleep-rumpled, lit by a lamp somewhere far below.

"Mom," the girl said, and her voice came a half-second late, thinned by the distance. She pulled a blanket up around her shoulders. "I miss you."

Varn's thumb moved across the edge of the screen as if she could touch the small face there. Behind her, the sun broke over the rim of the world and threw a band of white light across the ring, across her hands, across the glass. She did not look away from her daughter. "I miss you too," she said softly. "So much."

<!-- cell: markdown id="1a1ee7ba-e1b2-44fe-8105-ceb433bb21cf" -->

The plain lay rust-red and still beneath a sky the color of weak tea, the sun a small pale coin low over the rim of the basin. Dust hung in the thin air and settled slowly, as it always had. Across the flats stood the excavation site: a ring of floodlights on spindly masts, a cluster of pressurized modules, and three tracked machines with drill arms folded against their flanks like the forelegs of insects. Suited figures moved among them in the slow, bounding gait the low gravity demanded, checking cables, reading displays, signaling to one another with gloved hands while their voices crackled over the open channel.

"Ground's clear. Start the first bore."

The lead excavator shuddered and lowered its arm. The drill head bit into the regolith with a grinding whine that carried strangely in the thin atmosphere, more felt through the boots than heard. A plume of ochre dust rose and drifted east. The crew watched the depth counter climb: two meters, four, six. Beneath them, far down in the cold stone, something that had lain motionless for longer than the basin had held its shape registered the vibration. It did not move. It did not need to. It simply attended, the way a sleeper half-wakes at a sound in the house, and a slow unease spread through the vast lattice of its body. The tremors came closer. The rhythm did not stop.

At eleven meters the drill met resistance. The pitch of the whine rose to a shriek, and on the monitors the readout spiked. "Something dense down there. Not rock. It's... layered." The operator eased the pressure, then pushed again. With a crack that sent a jolt through every machine on the site, the bit punched through. Below, the exoskeleton, curved, ridged and hard as fired ceramic, split around the intruding steel, and the soft tissue beneath it flinched from the cold metal and the sudden pain.

Then every receiver at the site screamed. Static flooded the channel, then a pulse, deep and repeating, so strong it set the floodlight masts humming and made the suited figures clutch at their helmets. The instruments in the command module lit red as a signal unlike anything they had been built to read poured outward from beneath the plain, past the crew, past the basin rim, up through the thin brown sky, aimed at somewhere none of them could see. The drill sat buried and silent in the wound. The crew stood frozen in the dust, listening to the ground cry out.

<!-- cell: markdown id="bbe72df5-9970-4f36-b76c-47a8490f97dc" -->

The observation deck of the station turned slowly above the grey moon, and through its long curved window the surface rolled past in shades of ash and bone. Far below, where a crater wall had been cut open like a wound, lights burned in a tight cluster. From this height the excavators looked like insects at work on a carcass, their arms rising and falling, their treads leaving pale scars in the dust. A monitor beside the glass showed the same scene closer, relayed from the dig site: a shaft driven straight down, its walls braced with steel, and at its bottom a dark curve of something that was not stone.

Varn stood with her hands on the rail, her coat still sealed to the collar as though she had only just come in from the cold of the docking ring. Her daughter leaned against the glass beside her, breath fogging a small circle on its surface, watching the machines. Behind them the deck was nearly empty; a technician at the far console murmured figures into a headset and did not look up.

On the monitor, one of the great excavators shuddered and bit deeper, and a plume of dust drifted up the shaft in the low gravity, hanging there long after the scoop had withdrawn. Crews in pressure suits moved along the rim, small and careful, guiding cables down into the dark. The curve at the bottom of the hole grew a little wider, a little more exposed, smooth where the rock around it was rough.

"Nobody knows what it is," Varn said quietly, not taking her eyes from the window. Her daughter turned to look at her, and Varn nodded toward the lights below. "Not the engineers, not the survey teams, not anyone. And still they keep digging." She watched the excavator lower its arm again, and the deck hummed faintly around them as the station carried them on across the face of the moon.

<!-- cell: markdown id="35639bea-6278-4100-ab5f-2bf89bd544a6" -->

The dust storm had thinned by late afternoon, and the light that reached the floor of the canyon was the color of weak tea. Rust-red cliffs rose on either side, scoured smooth by wind older than any memory, and the ground between them was littered with broken basalt and the long shallow drifts of fine sand that hissed and shifted whenever the air stirred. Far overhead, the small pale sun hung in a sky the shade of old salmon, and nothing moved across the plain but the thin streamers of dust lifting from the ridgelines.

He lay pressed into a cleft at the base of the cliff, where an overhang of stone cast a wedge of cold shadow. He was not shaped like anything that belonged to that place: a dark, glistening mass that rippled faintly along its length, threaded with fine filaments that glowed and dimmed in slow pulses. One flank of him was torn, and a thick, pearly fluid had seeped out and frozen into a crust on the rock beneath. Deep within his body, something brightened, a steady point of violet light, and the air around him seemed to tighten, as though the space itself had been drawn taut like a membrane. The signal leapt outward from him and was gone, crossing a distance no light could cross, and almost at once an answer came back through the same impossible channel.

"State the nature of your emergency," the voice said. It arrived not as sound but as a pressure inside him, calm and precise, untroubled by the gulf between stars.

His filaments flared and flickered. "I was discovered," he answered, the words shaped from pulses of light and pressure. "They found me. They attacked." The torn place along his side shivered as he spoke, and he drew himself deeper into the shadow of the overhang, pressing against the stone. For a moment there was only the hiss of sand and the thin moan of wind through the canyon. Then the voice returned, steady and certain. "Stay where you are. Do not move. Help is on the way." The violet light within him dimmed to a low, patient glow, and he went still against the rock, watching the empty red plain as the shadows of the cliffs crept slowly across it.

<!-- cell: markdown id="83bfae0f-d9c1-446c-95f5-d7a71009aca8" -->

The field lay open under a pale sky, the soil turned dark and soft where the plough had last passed. Furrows ran in long, uneven lines toward a hedge of thorn at the far edge, and between them the earth gave off a cool, damp smell, the smell of roots and rain that had soaked in overnight. Crows picked at the clods some distance off, lifting and settling again without hurry.

Near the middle of the field, half sunk in the dirt, sat the object. It was the size of a cart wheel, smooth and dull grey, its surface unmarked by scratch or seam. The soil around it had been pushed outward in a shallow ring, as though it had come down hard and pressed the ground aside. Nothing grew on the ring, and the morning dew had not beaded on the thing's skin the way it had on every blade of grass nearby.

Varn stood a few paces back from it, his boots caked to the ankle, one broad hand resting on his daughter's shoulder. She was small beside him, her hair tied back with a strip of cloth, and she leaned forward to look at the object without letting go of his sleeve. For a long moment she studied it in silence, her breath clouding faintly in the cold.

"Is it dangerous?" she asked at last, glancing up at him.

Varn kept his eyes on the grey shape a moment longer before he looked down at her. His jaw was set, but his voice came out low and even. "Don't worry," he told her, and his hand tightened gently on her shoulder. The crows called somewhere behind them, and the wind moved through the thorn hedge, and the object sat in its ring of pressed earth, still and silent.

<!-- cell: markdown id="31fa4c96-f15b-4da2-988c-880d89fba93f" -->

High above Mars, the planet turned slow and rust-colored beneath a scattering of Earth ships, their hulls catching the hard white light of a distant sun. Beyond them the dark lay deep and empty, pricked with stars, the thin crescent of Phobos sliding along its track. Out past the far edge of that orbit, where nothing had been a moment before, the stars bent and shivered. Shapes came out of the black there, long and angular and wrong in their proportions, one after another, until a loose formation of alien ships hung in the void, silent and unlit, holding a far orbit around the red world.

Across the Earth fleet the alarms went up at once. Sensor stations flared amber, and in cramped compartments crews leaned toward their consoles, voices overlapping on open channels as contact reports stacked one on top of the next. Bearing, range, count. Someone called the number aloud and then called it again, higher, as more of the vessels resolved.

On the bridge of a corvette riding the inner track, Varn stood behind her sensor officer with one hand braced on the back of his chair. The main screen had been showing nothing but the curve of Mars and the faint glitter of the fleet's running lights. Now a cluster of new contacts bloomed along its outer rim, each tagged in red, each sitting far out where no ship of theirs should be. She watched them settle into place on the display, her jaw tight, the low hum of the corvette's systems the only sound for a long breath.

"Magnify," she said quietly. The screen zoomed, and the alien hulls filled it, dark and still against the stars. Around her the bridge crew had gone silent, every face turned to the same image.

<!-- cell: markdown id="ea16d49d-df31-44dc-908b-cefcd276cd8b" -->

The planet turned slowly beneath them, a pale disc banded with cloud, its night side scattered with nothing but dark. Two ships held station in high orbit above it. The larger was the lab ship, broad and ungainly, its hull studded with sensor blisters and docking collars, its long spine lit along every viewport where scientists still bent over their instruments. A few kilometers off, small against the curve of the world, hung the corvette: a narrow grey hull with a single turret on its dorsal ridge and a pair of point-defense mounts that had never been meant for anything heavier than debris and the occasional smuggler.

On the corvette's bridge the light was low and blue. The crew sat at their stations in the quiet hum of the air recyclers, watching telemetry crawl across their screens, until the communications officer straightened and turned in her chair. A priority signal had come in from Earth, relayed across the long chain of buoys, its header flagged in red. She put it through to the command station without a word.

Varn read it standing. The orders were brief and plain: the discovery was to be protected at all costs, the mission was to hold position, no further support could be dispatched in the near term. She read it a second time, and then she looked up through the forward port at the lab ship drifting beside them, at its rows of lit windows, and past it to the black where the jump lanes opened. She knew what the signal did not say. If anyone came for what lay in that ship's holds, they would not come with one vessel. They would come with a fleet, and one lightly armed corvette could not stop a fleet; it could only stand in front of one.

The bridge had gone silent around her. The helmsman kept his eyes on his console, and the gunnery officer's hand rested motionless beside the turret controls, both of them waiting. Varn closed the message and set her palm flat on the edge of the command station for a long moment. Then she ordered the sensor watch doubled, the point-defense crews brought to readiness, and a full sweep of the approach lanes run every hour, and she stayed where she was, facing the dark beyond the planet's rim, while her crew turned back to their work.

<!-- cell: markdown id="fffe04ff-f930-473e-bf79-939b2626e25d" -->

Earth hung below them in its slow blue turn, the terminator line creeping across the Pacific, and above it the picket of human ships held station in a loose crescent. Their hulls were scarred grey and white, running lights blinking in patient rhythm against the black. Beyond them, where there had been only stars an hour before, the other ships were coming. They did not glint the way metal should. Their surfaces drank the sunlight and gave back something oily and shifting, a dull sheen that crawled along curves that seemed to fold into themselves. There were no engine plumes, no visible drives, no seams or ports or antennae, only shapes that grew steadily larger, closing the distance with a smoothness that made the sensor operators lean closer to their screens.

On the bridge of the lead ship the air was close and quiet. Crew members bent over their consoles, tracking readings that refused to settle into anything familiar. Energy signatures rose and fell along the alien hulls in pulses no one could name: a charging capacitor, a reactor at idle, a weapon array coming online, or nothing more than the ships' equivalent of breathing. One analyst ran the pattern through every threat library on board and watched each comparison return empty. Another called out a spike along the nearest vessel's flank, then fell silent when it faded again just as quickly, meaning nothing anyone could prove.

Varn stood at the communications station with one hand braced on the edge of the console. He keyed the hail again, his voice level and deliberate. "Unidentified vessels, this is the Earth defense picket. Please respond." He waited. The channel hissed back at him, empty. He switched frequencies and sent it again, then again on the next band, then as a burst of raw data, mathematical primes, a simple repeating tone. Each time he paused and listened, his eyes fixed on the forward display where the dark shapes kept coming. Each time there was nothing, not a flicker, not a change in course, not the faintest echo of acknowledgment.

The crew around him had stopped pretending not to watch. Their attention divided itself between Varn's hands on the controls and the screens, where the strange hulls swelled to fill more of the view. Varn drew a breath, opened the channel once more, and spoke the same words into the silence. The alien ships offered no answer, and below them all the Earth went on turning.

<!-- cell: markdown id="d8769d0d-9629-4695-a660-62adf4120db8" -->

High above the planet's curve, the laboratory ship hung in the slow drift of orbit, its hull lit on one side by the sun and dark on the other. Through the forward viewports the alien ships could be seen at a distance, unmoving, their shapes too smooth and too strange to read as anything familiar. Inside, the lab deck was crowded and loud. Screens glowed along every wall, cycling through sensor sweeps and spectral charts, and the scientists moved between them in a hurried, restless rhythm, calling out numbers, leaning over one another's shoulders, pulling up old files beside new ones.

At the center of the deck, a cluster of them had gathered around the readings from the artifact. They laid its mineral scans side by side with the data streaming in from the alien hulls, and the lines matched: the same composition, the same trace signatures, the same apparent origin. One of them tapped the overlapping graphs and said that there could be little doubt now, that the ships had come for the artifact. Another shook her head and said that matching rock told them where something came from, not what anyone wanted with it. The sensors gave them metal and heat and radiation, and nothing that looked like a message. They kept working anyway, running the comparisons again, searching the noise for a pattern that was not there.

Varn stood at the edge of the deck with her arms folded, watching the ships through the viewport and then the people bent over their consoles. When she spoke, the nearest of them looked up. "What do they want?" she asked.

No one answered at once. The scientists exchanged glances, and the one by the scans spread his hands over the matching graphs. "We know they're connected to it," he said. "We don't know what they want. We can't tell you." Varn held his gaze a moment longer, then turned back to the viewport, where the alien ships waited, silent, against the dark.

<!-- cell: markdown id="3a1b3f3e-dd98-4af7-a50e-36359956a6d1" -->

The plain lay under a sky the color of weak tea, and the wind moved thin veils of rust-red dust across it without ever quite settling. In the middle of that emptiness the excavation had opened a wound in the ground: a broad, terraced pit cut down through layers of ochre and grey, ringed by pressurized domes, cable spools, and the squat bulk of the generators that thrummed through the soil. Floodlights on tall masts burned even in daylight, because the sun here was small and pale, and the work at the bottom of the pit could not wait for better light.

Down there the object waited too. Only part of it had been freed, a curved surface darker than anything around it, smooth in a way that stone never was, its edges still disappearing into the packed regolith. Crews in dust-caked suits worked along the terraces in shifts that overlapped so the digging never stopped. Some guided the arms of small excavators, shaving away the soil a few centimeters at a time; others knelt with brushes and handheld scanners where the machines were too clumsy, their helmet lamps throwing small circles of white across the black surface. Buckets of spoil rose on a creaking conveyor to the rim, where the wind took the finest grains and scattered them across the plain.

In the largest dome, past the airlock and its hiss of equalizing pressure, the scientists crowded around banks of screens. The readings from the pit arrived in a steady stream: density maps, spectral scans, faint patterns of energy that rose and fell with no rhythm anyone had yet named. One of them traced a line on a display with a gloved finger and said, without looking up, "The markings on the newest section match the signal structure. Same intervals." Another leaned in, pulled up a second window, and laid the two side by side. On a separate screen, kept always in view, a cluster of points crept across a star chart, the fleet that was coming, its distance updated every few minutes in small, unforgiving numbers.

No one in the dome spoke of slowing down. They spoke instead of what the object might be, whether it had been left as a beacon or a warning or a key, and what it could tell them about the ships that drew nearer with every hour. Through the dome's narrow viewport the floodlights blazed over the pit, the suited figures bent to their work, and the dark curve in the earth showed a little more of itself than it had the hour before.

<!-- cell: markdown id="94de66c2-ba6b-48b5-bd8b-e55ab1288bfa" -->

The observation deck hung on the night side of the station, and through its long curved window the planet below was only a darker shape against the dark, its edge traced by a thin blue line of atmosphere. The lights had been dimmed for the station's sleep cycle. Air hissed softly through the vents, and somewhere deep in the hull a pump ticked with the patience of something that never stopped. Beyond the glass, the stars did not twinkle; they simply burned, hard and fixed.

Varn sat on the padded ledge beneath the window with her daughter beside her, the girl's knees drawn up and her bare feet tucked under the hem of a borrowed jacket far too large for her. Varn had not turned on the deck's display. Instead she pointed, her finger resting against the cold glass, toward a patch of sky just above the planet's rim where three faint points of light moved in a slow, deliberate line, too steady to be stars and too bright to be debris.

"Those are the ships," she said quietly. "They came in from past the outer moons four days ago. Nobody sent them, and nobody has been able to talk to them. They don't answer anything." The girl leaned closer to the window, her breath fogging a small circle on the glass, and watched the lights crawl along their path. Varn kept her eyes on them too. "They've been circling here, watching, I think. Mapping. And they keep turning, just a little, every time they come around." She was silent for a moment, and her hand found her daughter's shoulder. "I'm afraid that when they're finished here, they'll go on to Earth."

The girl did not say anything at first. She only pressed her palm flat against the window, as if she could cover the three lights and hold them still. Varn drew her in against her side, and the two of them sat without speaking while the ships slid on, unhurried, toward the curve of the world and the far blue speck beyond it that was home.

<!-- cell: markdown id="a28f9aa1-b0a2-44d4-bd6c-cfaea159f2e3" -->

The plain lay rust-red and silent under a sky the color of weak tea, its dust lifted now and then into thin, wandering veils by a wind too faint to be heard. Boulders stood half-buried where they had rolled a billion years before, each casting a long blue shadow toward the east. Far off, the rim of a crater rose like a low wall against the horizon, and above it, small and pale, the sun hung without warmth.

At the center of the plain rested the object. It was dark and smooth, larger than a man and shaped like nothing that had been made for walking or for flight, and a skin of fine red grit had settled into the seams along its flank. For a long while it did not move. Then a light woke somewhere within it, faint at first, then steady, pulsing a slow amber through the grit, and a low hum rose from its body and trembled the pebbles on the ground around it.

Overhead, near the zenith, a scatter of bright points crossed the sky in loose formation: the fleet, holding orbit. The object turned some part of itself toward them, and the hum sharpened into a voice that went out on every channel at once. "This is the object on the surface below you," it said, the words even and deliberate, but strained at their edges. "I am in need of dire assistance. I cannot hold much longer. Answer me. Come down." It paused, the amber light flickering, and then said it again, and again, each time a little slower.

High above, aboard the ships, the crews bent over their consoles as the signal poured in, one officer reaching to raise the volume while another pulled the source up on a display: a single bright mark on the red curve of the planet. On the plain, the dust drifted across the object's flank, and its light went on pulsing, patient and failing, toward the sky.

<!-- cell: markdown id="9da802b8-00f1-49fd-97c6-066888b457d7" -->

Earth turned below the cockpit glass, blue and white and enormous, its night side scattered with the amber lace of cities. Above it, where the sunlight fell hard and colorless, the small ship hung motionless against the dark, its running lights dimmed to a single red pulse. Beyond its bow, still distant but growing, a field of pale points moved in formation against the stars: too regular for constellations, too bright for debris. The fleet came on in silence, as everything did up here.

Varn sat strapped into the pilot's couch with her helmet resting on her knees. The console threw a soft green glow across her face, and the readouts scrolled their patient numbers: closing velocity, intercept window, fuel remaining. She did not look at them for long. Her eyes kept returning to the blue curve of the planet, to a coastline she seemed to be tracing from memory, and her jaw was set the way it is set when a decision has already been made and only the speaking of it remains.

She reached forward and opened the message channel. A small light blinked, waiting. For a while she said nothing at all, and the recorder captured only the hum of the cabin air and the tick of cooling metal. Then she leaned toward the pickup. "It's me," she said, and her voice held steady. "I don't have long, so listen. That fleet is coming for Earth, and if nobody gets in its way, there won't be anything left down there for you to grow up into. Somebody has to stand in front of it. I'm the one who's here." She stopped and pressed her knuckles against her mouth, then lowered her hand. "I want you to have a chance. A real one. That's all this is. That's all I ever wanted for you."

She looked at the planet again, and her voice softened. "Be brave, but don't be stupid about it. Be kind when it costs you something. And when you look up at night, don't look for me. Look at what's still there. I love you. Goodbye, sweetheart." She held her finger over the control a moment longer, as if there were one more thing, and then she pressed it. The light changed from amber to green, and the message went out, a thin thread of signal falling toward the blue world below, while ahead of the little ship the pale points of the fleet kept growing.

<!-- cell: markdown id="0035b0c9-39e1-4fcc-9cc3-af4f53b50394" -->

Above the curve of the planet, where the dayside glare thinned into a blue rind of atmosphere, the object hung in its slow tumble, and around it the attackers swarmed like sparks thrown off a grinding wheel. Their small craft darted in and out of its shadow, scoring its hull with bursts of light that bloomed and faded without sound. Farther out, beyond the reach of that bright and silent violence, the fleet lay in a long, unhurried line, its great ships turned edge-on to the sun so that only their running lights showed against the dark.

Aboard the lead vessel, the watch crew bent over their displays as the sensor sweeps came back. Contacts resolved one by one into outlines and drive signatures: the swarm at the object, and beyond it a scattering of other ships riding higher orbits, keeping their distance. An analyst traced the pattern of their emissions against the attackers' and spoke without looking up. "Same hull lineage. Same drive harmonics. They're one tribe." Down the line of consoles the classification passed from station to station, and the markers on the plot changed color, every ship in orbit now tagged alike, the distant ones no different from the ones tearing at the object. The fleet's heading shifted by a fraction, and the line began to close.

Then one marker broke from the rest. A lone corvette, scarred and narrow, its engines flaring white, swung out of its high orbit and dropped straight across the fleet's path. In its cramped cockpit Varn held the controls steady with both hands, her jaw set, the vast shapes of the fleet swelling in her forward screen until they filled it. She did not slow. She did not look away. Somewhere behind her, her daughter needed every second she could buy, and Varn meant to put her small ship and herself between the fleet and that need for as long as she could.

On the lead vessel the plot flickered as the corvette's vector cut across their own, and the watch crew watched it come, a single bright point drawing a line across the dark, squarely in their way.

<!-- cell: markdown id="41e5f39d-49c1-48da-a186-7819b9a38358" -->

Above the curve of the world, where the day line crept across cloud and ocean, the ships hung in their slow parking orbits: freighters with their cargo spines folded, tenders nosed up against them, a scattering of patrol craft riding the same invisible track. Their running lights blinked in patient sequence against the black. On their bridges, crews worked through the quiet routines of a watch that had promised to be uneventful, logging fuel figures, adjusting attitude by fractions of a degree, glancing now and then at the planet turning beneath them.

Then the attack came. Lances of light cut across the orbital lanes, and the first freighter came apart along its spine, its halves tumbling away from each other in a spray of frozen atmosphere. Alarms shrieked on every deck still standing. A tender tried to pull free of its moorings and was struck mid-burn; its reactor flared white and left only an expanding ring of debris. Crews shouted over one another on open channels, some calling for evasive vectors, some simply calling names. Ship after ship blossomed and went dark, and the orderly procession of lights became a field of drifting wreckage glittering in the sunlight.

Through that wreckage the fleet answered. Its ships wheeled into line with their batteries already warm, and on their bridges gunnery officers leaned over their consoles, calling out solutions as the targeting grids filled with red. Varn's corvette was in their sights: a lean, dark hull slipping between the shattered freighters, its engines burning hard. The order went out, and the fleet fired as one.

The volley found the corvette broadside. For an instant its shields threw back a pale shimmer; then they failed, and the hull buckled inward before it burst. Fire raced along its length and was snuffed out at once by the vacuum, leaving a scatter of glowing fragments spinning outward among the other ruins. On the fleet's bridges, the crews watched the last trace of it fade from their screens, and for a long moment no one spoke, while below them the planet went on turning, its clouds untouched.

<!-- cell: markdown id="a2483d56-4aac-43fb-a99e-c7780c53d2c8" -->

The plain lay wide and rust-colored under a sky the shade of weak tea, its horizon blurred by a haze of fine dust that never quite settled. Low ridges of broken basalt ran off toward the west, and the sun, small and pale, threw long shadows across the ground. Here and there the regolith had been cut open in neat terraces, the exposed layers banded in ochre, gray and a deeper, almost violet red where the digging had gone furthest down.

Across the terraces the automated excavators worked in a loose line, squat machines on broad treads with jointed arms and toothed buckets. Their movements were patient and exact: a bucket lowered, bit into the slope, lifted a load of soil and swung it aside onto a growing spoil heap, then lowered again. Thin plumes of dust rose behind each one and drifted sideways in the faint wind. There was almost no sound in the thin air, only a dull vibration that passed through the ground beneath the treads.

Then, one after another, the excavators stopped. A bucket halted halfway through its swing and hung there, a trickle of red grit sliding from its lip. The next machine drew its arm back and went still. Down the line the rest followed, treads locked, arms frozen at odd angles against the sky, until not one of them moved. The little plumes of dust thinned, drifted, and settled over the cut ground.

The plain fell entirely quiet. The status lamps on the machines' flanks glowed steadily in the dim light, and the wind went on pushing fine dust in small, unhurried ripples across the terraces, past the motionless treads, and over the edge of the open trench.

<!-- cell: markdown id="2808d38c-08b2-460f-9bd1-e3955b881ba9" -->

The operations floor beneath Geneva had been quiet for most of the night shift, its long rows of consoles glowing a steady blue. Then, one after another, the transponder signals of the fleet stopped. There was no distress call, no debris field on the long-range scopes, no gradual fading of power. One moment the icons hung in their careful formation at the edge of the system; the next the screens showed only empty space and a column of timestamps where the telemetry had ended. A technician stood up so fast her chair rolled into the aisle. Someone called for a recheck of the relay network, and someone else answered, too loudly, that the relays were fine, that every one of them was fine.

Within minutes the room filled. Officers in half-buttoned uniforms crowded around the central display while analysts read out the same numbers again and again, as if repetition might change them. Nobody believed that so many ships could fail together by accident. A general with grey stubble on his jaw said the word first, flatly: "Attack." The room took it up. Orders went out to raise the planetary defenses, to wake the reserve crews, to alert every orbital platform. The clocks on the wall turned over to a new alert level, and the blue light of the consoles gave way to red.

Far from the command bunkers, in a small apartment on the fourteenth floor of a residential tower, a young woman sat at her kitchen table with a cup of tea gone cold beside her. The terminal on the wall chimed once, a delayed packet routed through the deep-space relays, stamped with her mother's name: Varn. She opened it without thinking, and her mother's face filled the screen, lit by the harsh white light of a ship's interior, her hair pulled back, her eyes steady.

"I don't have long," Varn said, and her voice was calm in a way her daughter had only heard a few times in her life. "Whatever you hear about us, I want you to know I was thinking of you. I'm proud of you. Be brave, and don't let anyone tell you what to believe about what happened out here." She paused, glanced at something off-screen, and looked back with the trace of a smile. "I love you. Take care of yourself." The image froze on that last look. Outside the window, the city's warning sirens began to rise, one district after another, and the daughter sat without moving, her hand pressed flat against the edge of the table.
